course: dictionary
lesson: 3638
title: Finite: having an end
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Finite means having an end or a limit. Something finite does not go on forever. «Definitely comes from finite, meaning having an end.» Hidden inside definitely is finite, so a little helper is de-finite-ly: the word finite is in the middle.
explain: Think of the coal in a bag, the oil in a tank, or the apples in a basket. Each one is finite, because it can all be used up. Numbers are different: they never stop, so we call them infinite.
explain: Finite has a second meaning in grammar. A finite verb shows tense (when the action happens) and agrees with its subject, as in «she runs». Not every verb form can do this, so grammar has finite and non-finite verbs.
fact: Finite means having an end or a limit, not going on forever.
fact: A finite set in maths has a number of things you can count all the way to the end.
fact: Numbers themselves are infinite, because they never stop.
fact: In science and geography, finite resources like oil and coal will run out.
fact: A finite verb shows tense and agrees with its subject, as in «she runs».
exercise: «Coal is a finite resource, so one day it will all be used up.» What does finite mean here? | *Having an end, so it will run out | Very shiny | Going on forever | Made of wood
exercise: Which of these is a finite set, something you can count to the end? | The sky | All the counting numbers, 1, 2, 3 and on forever | *The ten apples in a basket | Every number there could ever be
exercise: In «she runs», the verb runs is a finite verb. What does finite mean for a verb? | *It shows when the action happens and matches its subject | It never ends | It has no tense at all | It is always a noun
test: Which of these will run out one day, because it is finite? | Every whole number there is | The numbers you can ever count to | Sunlight that never stops | *The coal in a bag
test: Which sentence has a finite verb? | Running in the park | *The cat sleeps all day | The dog barking loudly | To run fast is fun
exam: What does finite mean in «Definitely comes from finite, meaning having an end.»? | Bright and shiny | Going on for ever | Very loud | *Having an end or a limit
